description:
Since `lazy_()` merely a constructor for lazy objects, this adds an actual standard evaluation version of `lazy()`.

One use case is to check whether arguments of programmatically created functions are missing. For example, this is useful for signalling to the user that a default value has been chosen when she doesn't explicitly provide arguments:

``` r
 check_explicitness <- function(input, execution_env) {
  l <- lazyeval::lazy__(as.symbol(input), execution_env)
  if (identical(l$env, execution_env)) {
    message("Picking default value for '", input, "': ", get(input, execution_env))
  }
}

walk(inputs_names, check_explicitness, env)
```
